#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514} :activity/id #uuid "0c5859d1-4ff1-5efe-90ef-d19961e8bcc4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048896} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088514 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088889 :tutorial/content "In mathematics, rectangular arrays containing numbers are called **matrices**. \n\nAs an example, consider the matrix $A$ below:\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis particular matrix has $3$ rows and $3$ columns. The numbers in the matrix are called **entries** or **elements.**\n\nThe number of rows and columns in a matrix does not need to be the same. For example, consider another matrix that has $3$ rows and $4$ columns:\n\n$$\nM = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 13 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe **dimensions** of the matrix are the number of rows and the number of columns. For example, the matrix $M$ shown above has $3$ rows and $4$ columns. So, we write the dimensions of $M$ as $3 \\times 4$ (read as \"three by four\")." :tutorial/id #uuid "d68f01b8-8bc6-59a1-8d80-d284abbcdf6e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2968 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "22697b73-625d-545a-ba00-223291b29933" :step/math-academy-id 16069 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087693 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087500 :question/id #uuid "6587b5f1-8a44-556a-a95e-20c54dfebc04" :question/math-academy-id "e-4367" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${A},$ where \n\n$$\n{A}=\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 5 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 6 \\\\ 3 & 2 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${A}$ has $4$ rows and $3$ columns. Therefore, its dimensions are $4\\times 3.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6ae25fc-16eb-598b-a288-742e4b46cb12" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050404}] :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616360} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235398} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259822} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259823} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259824} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299741} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616348} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186616356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186625550}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "b1b7d3eb-f611-589c-99d3-92b8c28fd2c4" :step/math-academy-id 16070 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087694 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087501 :question/id #uuid "74a9046e-e0a3-59d6-8b13-437e9a0cf04a" :question/math-academy-id "e-4370" :question/problem "What are the dimensions of the matrix ${D},$ where \n\n$$\n{D}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 1 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The matrix ${D}$ has $1$ row and $4$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1\\times 4.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34fd903a-fa34-57b0-a098-08fa1cbd4f95" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617120} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088350} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259826} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299742} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617095} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617103} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617104}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Dimensions of a Matrix with One Row or Column"} :step/id #uuid "17d76850-618a-5b25-88fc-fc5296c2deb2" :step/math-academy-id 16073 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088522 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088891 :tutorial/content "Some matrices have only one column, for instance\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 13\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is a $2\\times 1$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 1$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 1$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **column vectors**.\n\nOther matrices have only one row, for instance\n\n$$\nD = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nE = \\begin{bmatrix}13 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nF = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nHere, $D$ is a $1\\times 2$ matrix, $E$ is a $1\\times 3$ matrix, and $F$ is a $1\\times 4$ matrix. These types of matrices are often called **row vectors**.\n\n**Note:** Every number can be thought of as a $1\\times 1$ matrix. For instance, we can consider the number $5$ as the matrix\n\n$$\n{G}\n=\\begin{bmatrix}\\,5\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1628e679-983a-5be7-9af6-81f0f45fa29a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2984 :tutorial/title "Matrices With One Row or Column"} :step/id #uuid "1f0e819b-30a9-5921-8910-450a2910edb7" :step/math-academy-id 16114 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088516}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088534 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088898 :tutorial/content "A **square matrix** is a matrix where the number of rows is equal to the number of columns.\n\nFor example, the matrix\n\n<div class=\"matrix-operands matrix-operands-stacked\">\n<div>$A =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nis a square matrix because it has $2$ rows and $2$ columns. Its dimensions are $2 \\times 2.$\n\nIn general, the dimensions of a square matrix are $n \\times n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "06286713-0de9-5c18-959f-04de81bee510" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3115 :tutorial/title "Square Matrices"} :step/id #uuid "15b361ac-babf-50db-b55f-4a4ad993eb69" :step/math-academy-id 16855 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088515}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087713 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087511 :question/id #uuid "1fcce090-8441-5547-ab52-18b0ff41ac5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-5017" :question/problem "Which of the following is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix?\n\n$$\nA = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1.1 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nB = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 0 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n$$\nC = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- The matrix $A$ has $2$ rows and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $2 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\n- The matrix $B$ has $3$ rows and $2$ columns. So, its dimensions are $3 \\times 2.$ $\\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}$\n\n- The matrix $C$ has $1$ row and $3$ columns. So, its dimensions are $1 \\times 3.$ $\\quad{\\color{red}\\times}$\n\nTherefore, only $B$ is a $3 \\times 2$ matrix."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed7286fe-b6e2-5544-8bc6-86afc6cdb813" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186657695} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259827} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400184} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476067} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476094} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476096} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476097} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617138} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617141} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617164} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617168} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186617208}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Matrix by Its Description"} :step/id #uuid "b0393a9e-8102-5d2d-8b20-ac2709992291" :step/math-academy-id 18438 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088725 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088984 :tutorial/content "Matrices arise naturally in *many* real-world problems. Weather forecasting, image processing and computer graphics, video games, and online customization algorithms are just a few of the many applications of matrices. \n\nLet's consider an example where we can use a matrix to model bus routes in a city. \n\nThe diagram below shows the bus routes between three stations (Station A, Station B, and Station C) in a particular city, where $A, B,$ and $C$ represent the stations and the arrows represent the directed routes between them. \n\n![](images/cb/cbdf5c2470229ae3ba10ee18bccd2dc764407897397466b157474bf2b819f5d9.png)\n\nNote that the circles $A,B,$ and $C$ in our diagram are called **nodes**.\n\nWe wish to represent this same diagram using a matrix. Let's start by putting this information into a table like the one below:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nLet's now fill in the rows of our table. We begin by considering all of the bus routes from Station A to the other stations:\n\n- There are $\\color{blue}0$ bus routes from $A$ to itself.\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $B.$\n\n- There is $\\color{blue}1$ bus route from $A$ to $C.$\n\nFilling in the first row of the table, we get the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\color{blue} 0$</td><td>$\\color{blue} 1$</td><td>$\\color{blue} 1$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td></td><td></td><td></td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nFilling in the remaining rows in the same way, we get:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nFinally, keeping only the essential information, we obtain the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "523d25fd-6b49-5cd7-ac05-80ac6fa0f3d8" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6114 :tutorial/title "Using Matrices for Modeling"} :step/id #uuid "f1c6aa85-8a73-5586-afeb-0d6c77ff0b9a" :step/math-academy-id 37144 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088726 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087654 :question/id #uuid "5c3b6d53-3dbb-59f6-8d15-a9e6f48927a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-9942" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9af4801abdec165de2b720a21dd6a727c578e32cbae9c9c54bd589a1b65043b0.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows train routes between three train stations, where the nodes represent stations and arrows represent the directed routes between them. The (incomplete) route matrix below represents the number of routes between the three stations. Find the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}a}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}b}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}c}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our diagram.\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station A and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $A$ to $A,$ and so $a=1.$\n\n- No arrows are starting at Station B and ending at Station B. So, there are no routes from $B$ to $B,$ and so $b=0.$\n\n- There is $1$ arrow starting at Station C and ending at Station A. So, there is only one route from $C$ to $A,$ and so $c=1.$\n\nAs a result, our route matrix is the following:\n\n<div class=\"route-matrix\">\n  <span class=\"matrix-to\">To</span>\n  <span class=\"matrix-from\">From</span>\n  <table aria-label=\"Route matrix: rows are From stations; columns are To stations\">\n    <thead>\n      <tr><td class=\"matrix-corner\"></td><th scope=\"col\">A</th><th scope=\"col\">B</th><th scope=\"col\">C</th></tr>\n    </thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">A</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$1$</td><td>$0$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">B</th><td>$0$</td><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0}$</td><td>$2$</td></tr>\n      <tr><th scope=\"row\">C</th><td>$\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}1}$</td><td>$0$</td><td>$1$</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\na+b+c = 1+0+1 = 2.\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "10ca3efc-7d23-5bc1-ab41-f785bed69986" :knowledge-point/question-generator {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618084} :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088004} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088005} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271964} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186271965} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299755} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186299760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476121} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476124} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476134} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476153} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476154} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476161} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476170} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186476172} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618050} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618079} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618862} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618881} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618888} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186618893}] :knowledge-point/title "Matrices for Route Planning"} :step/id #uuid "e1afb939-c40e-55de-8823-355f35294017" :step/math-academy-id 37441}] :activity/title "Introduction to Matrices" :activity/type {:db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087928}]}
